<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-s03e08" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Episode 8: Adding/Subtracting Fractions, Part 1</title>
    <assemblage>
        <title>Episode 8 Learning Goals</title>
        <p>
         <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Add and subtract fractions that have the same denominator.
                </p>
            </li>
         </ul>   
        </p>
    </assemblage>
    <p>
        <alert>How to Add/Subtract Fraction with the Same Denominator</alert>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Add/Subtract the numerators and put the result over the common denominator
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Simplify your answer to lowest terms (if possible).
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </p>
    <example>
        <p>
            Add:
            <md>
                \frac{1}{3}+\frac{1}{3}
            </md>
        </p>
        <p>
            Solution: The fractions have the same denominator, 3. Add the two numbers and put the result over the common denominator, 3:
            <md>
                \frac{1+1}{3} = \frac{2}{3}
            </md>
            This cannot be further simplified, so 2/3 is our final answer.
        </p>
    </example>
    <p>
        <alert>Makes Sense, Right?</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
        If we have 1/3 of a pizza and we combine it with another 1/3 of a pizza, we have end up 2/3 of a pizza.
    </p>
    <figure>
        <caption>1/3 of a pizza combined with another 1/3 of a pizza gives 2/3 of a pizza.</caption>
        <image width="90%">
            <prefigure label="pf-two-thirds-pizza-2" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
            <diagram dimensions="(425,100)" margins="5">
            <coordinates bbox="(0,0,17,4)">
            <arc center="(2,2)" radius="2"  sector="yes" fill="green" range="(90,210)"/>
            <arc center="(8,2)" radius="2"  sector="yes" fill="green" range="(210,330)"/>
            <arc center="(15,2)" radius="2"  sector="yes" fill="green" range="(9,210)"/>
            <arc center="(15,2)" radius="2"  sector="yes" fill="green" range="(210,330)"/>
            <circle center="(2,2)" radius="2" stroke="black" thickness="4"/>
            <circle center="(8,2)" radius="2" stroke="black" thickness="4"/>
            <circle center="(15,2)" radius="2" stroke="black" thickness="4"/>
            <line endpoints="((4.5,2), (5.5,2))" stroke="purple" thickness="2"/>
            <line endpoints="((5,1.5), (5,2.5))" stroke="purple" thickness="2"/>
            <line endpoints="((11,1.9), (12,1.9))" stroke="purple" thickness="2"/>
            <line endpoints="((11,2.1), (12,2.1))" stroke="purple" thickness="2"/>
            <line endpoints="((2,2), (2,4))" stroke="black" thickness="4"/>
            <line endpoints="((2,2), (0.268,1))" stroke="black" thickness="4"/>
            <line endpoints="((2,2), (3.732,1))" stroke="black" thickness="4"/>
            <line endpoints="((8,2), (8,4))" stroke="black" thickness="4"/>
            <line endpoints="((8,2), (6.268,1))" stroke="black" thickness="4"/>
            <line endpoints="((8,2), (9.732,1))" stroke="black" thickness="4"/>
            <line endpoints="((15,2), (15,4))" stroke="black" thickness="4"/>
            <line endpoints="((15,2), (13.268,1))" stroke="black" thickness="4"/>
            <line endpoints="((15,2), (16.732,1))" stroke="black" thickness="4"/>
            </coordinates>
            </diagram>
            </prefigure>   
        </image>
        </figure>
        <p>
            That's why <m>\frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{3} = \frac{2}{3}</m>
        </p>
    <example>
        <p>
            Subtract:
            <md>
                \frac{3}{4}-\frac{1}{4}
            </md>
        </p>
        <p>
            Solution: The two fractions have the same denominator, 4. So we subtract the two numerators and put the result over the common denominator:
            <md>
                \frac{3-1}{4} = \frac{2}{4}
            </md>
            We notice that 2/4 can be simplified:
            <md>
                \frac{2\div 2}{4\div 2} = \frac{1}{2}
            </md>
        </p>
    </example>
    <p>
        When we're adding and subtracting negative fractions, the same rules apply when we were adding subtracting negative whole numbers in Season 2.
    </p>
    <example>
        <p>
            Calculate:
            <md>
                -\frac{3}{5} + \frac{1}{5}
            </md>
        </p>
        <p>
            Solution: We are adding two numbers together, one positive, one negative. So, we find the difference between the two numbers:
            <md>
                \frac{3}{5}-\frac{1}{5} = \frac{3-1}{5} = \frac{2}{5}
            </md>
            Then we remember that in our original problem, the negative number <m>\frac{3}{5}</m> was bigger. So, that means the final answer will also be negative:
            <md>
                -\frac{2}{5}
            </md>
        </p>
    </example>

    <example>
        <p>
            Calculate
            <md>
                \frac{8}{7}-\left(-\frac{6}{7}\right)
            </md>
        </p>
        <p>
            Solution: We are subtracting a negative number, so we add the opposite:
            <md>
                \frac{8}{7}+\frac{6}{7} = \frac{8+6}{7} = \frac{14}{7} = 2
            </md>
        </p>
    </example>
